SEEDER_PATH = "duty_board/academy_seed_accounts_pro.py"
CHECK_ONLY = "--check" in sys.argv

L = lambda t, est, html: {"title": t, "est": est, "html": html}
Q = lambda q, opts, ans, why, src: {"q": q, "opts": opts, "ans": ans, "why": why, "src": src}

LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — The close as ritual, the statements as testimony", "13", "<p>Six modules built machinery; this module is its destination. The <b>period close</b> — the monthly ritual that seals a month's books — and the <b>financial statements</b> — the testimony those sealed books give — are what everything upstream exists FOR: every bridge, sweep, and reconciliation the track installed was, all along, a line on the close checklist being built one module at a time. This chapter is the frame; the module is the assembly.</p><p><b>What a close IS.</b> The close is the disciplined sequence that takes a month from trading to SEALED: all its documents in (cut-off — Chapter 3), all its rituals run (the sweeps, bridges, and reconciliations — Chapter 2 lists them), its manual layer completed (accruals, the journal audit), its truth verified (the trial balance scan, the tie checks), and its past frozen (the freeze advance — Inventory 8's ordered ritual, now at its home). A closed month is a month whose statements can be circulated without a disclaimer — <b>reported truth that will stay reported</b> — and the close's product is exactly that confidence, manufactured monthly.</p><p><b>Why MONTHLY is the whole argument.</b> The alternative — trading all year, closing once — is the year-end scramble every consultant has witnessed: twelve months of stragglers, unswept suspense, unreconciled banks, and unaccrued costs, compressed into January's archaeology, producing statements nobody trusts about a year nobody remembers. The monthly close dissolves the scramble by the academy's oldest trade (the storm into weather): each month's work is small BECAUSE last month's was done, every error is caught while its evidence is fresh, and December becomes the twelfth boring month — the phrase that will close this module's law. The economics are the compounding kind three tracks have priced: a firm's first monthly close takes days (the backlog's tail); its sixth takes hours; the year-end of a monthly-closing firm is a formality plus an audit that samples instead of excavates.</p><p><b>What statements ARE.</b> The three testimonies, previewed for their chapters: the <b>P&amp;L</b> — what the period earned and consumed (Chapter 4); the <b>balance sheet</b> — what the firm has and owes at the sealed moment, every line an assertion this academy taught the machinery behind (Chapter 5); the <b>cash flow</b> — where the money actually went, the statement that answers the owner's eternal confusion (Chapter 6). Their authority derives entirely from the close beneath them: statements printed from an unclosed month are drafts wearing suits, and the consultant's discipline is never to let a client circulate them as otherwise — the close IS the signature.</p><p><b>The reader this module trains.</b> One more purpose, honestly stated: most trading-firm owners have never been TAUGHT to read their statements — they receive them, nod, and file them — and the consultant who reads the three pages aloud with the MD monthly (Chapters 4-6 are the script) is delivering the implementation's final product: not software, not even discipline, but an owner who can hear their own firm's testimony. That reading habit, installed, outlives every other deliverable.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The monthly close checklist: the assembly", "14", "<p>The track's rituals, assembled into their sequence. One list, owned lines, run in order — the module's central artifact, and the page every implementation leaves behind.</p><p><b>The sequence, with its logic.</b> Order matters because later lines verify earlier ones:</p><ul><li><b>1. Cut-off sweep</b> — the month's documents IN: unsubmitted drafts hunted (deliveries picked but unposted, receipts at the gate, invoices pending), the straggler paperwork posted with honest dates (module 2's batched, announced sweep), the To-Bill queues read (both directions — the unbilled delivery and the unbilled receipt are next month's margin lies). Chapter 3 gives this line its own chapter because it earns one.</li><li><b>2. The manual layer</b> — the recurring set run (accruals, prepayment consumptions, payroll, depreciation — module 2's templates), reversals verified, the month's journal audit (the five questions, the count, the aggregate tells).</li><li><b>3. The sweeps</b> — suspense to zero (each squatter classified home), the unaddressed dimension lump to its true addresses (module 6), unallocated payments and unapplied credits worked (module 4's read).</li><li><b>4. The reconciliations</b> — every bank against its statement (module 3's ritual, the residues named and aged), the cash counts confirmed current.</li><li><b>5. The bridges</b> — stock-to-accounts to the kobo (Inventory 5's certificate), the tax registers to their accounts (module 5's pre-filing gate, whether or not a filing falls this week), controls to parties (module 4's tie check, first line of its own ritual).</li><li><b>6. The subsidiary rituals' outputs received</b> — the AR/AP ritual's artifacts (aging, provisions moved, advance schedules), the tax cycle's zero-verifications, the stock month-end's health page inputs — the other calendars' close-facing products, collected not re-performed.</li><li><b>7. The trial balance scan</b> — module 1's ten minutes: anomalies flagged and worked (wrong-side balances, should-be-zeros with residents, the unrecognised line).</li><li><b>8. The freeze advance</b> — stragglers in, verified, THEN the period locked (accounts and stock together — Inventory 8's lockstep), the narrow door's month logged.</li><li><b>9. Statements produced and READ</b> — the three testimonies printed from the sealed month, the MD reading held (Chapters 4-6), the close bundle filed (checklist signed, artifacts attached — the month's folder joining the audit file Chapter 8 will hand over whole).</li></ul><p><b>Owners and rhythm.</b> Every line a name (the sizing law: at eleven people, three names cover the list; at scale, the list maps a team); the close CALENDAR fixed (working days 1-5 as the working target — the close that drifts to day 15 is reporting on a month nobody remembers); and the checklist itself versioned on the policy page — lines added as the firm grows (the new tax, the new dimension), never removed without the memo that says why. The first close is the engagement's summit (the backlog paid down once); every close after is the descent path this list makes routine — and the consultant's stabilisation-period habit transfers verbatim from module 4: attend the first three, then audit the fourth from its file alone. A close whose file tells the whole story without its author in the room has graduated.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Cut-off: the period boundary's discipline", "13", "<p>The checklist's first line earns its chapter because cut-off errors are the close's most common disease and the P&amp;L's most direct lie: a document in the wrong period moves REAL margin between months, and nobody edits anything to make it happen — omission does it alone.</p><p><b>The completeness pairs.</b> Cut-off is about PAIRS of documents completing within the period, and the posting map (module 1) names them: <b>deliveries and their invoices</b> — a period's revenue is its invoices, its COGS is its deliveries (the timeline law's accounting face), so the month whose deliveries posted but invoices lagged carries COGS without revenue (margin understated — this month lies low, next month lies high when the invoices land), and the mirror month overstates; the To-Bill (sales) queue at close IS the exposure, read in naira, and the close either bills it or knowingly carries it with the accountant's note. <b>Receipts and their bills</b> — the RNB accrual (module 1's diagnostic gem) holds the purchase side's cut-off honestly BY DESIGN (possession booked at receipt; the liability accrues whether or not the supplier's invoice arrived — the machinery doing automatically what manual books needed adjusting entries for), and the close's job is the RNB's AGE read: an old RNB balance is invoices not being processed, which is a discipline problem wearing an accrual. <b>Consumptions and their accruals</b> — the utilities consumed unbilled, the services received uninvoiced: module 2's accrual rhythm, run as checklist line 2.</p><p><b>The document sweep's craft.</b> The hunt for the month's missing paper: draft documents listed (every doctype, filtered unsubmitted, dated this month — the five-minute query that finds most of it), the operational queues read (the warehouse's unposted receipts, the billing desk's pending list), the recurring known-latecomers anticipated (the supplier who always bills on the 5th for the prior month — accrued by template, reversed on arrival), and the sweep TIMED: day one of the close, so the stragglers post with honest in-period dates before the freeze conversation begins (the sweep-then-freeze order, again — the close that freezes first manufactures its own next-month stragglers).</p><p><b>Why this flows straight to margin — the teaching example.</b> The consultant's whiteboard minute for every client accountant: a ₦2m delivery on the 30th, invoiced on the 2nd. Cut-off kept: the delivery's COGS (say ₦1.5m) posts in month one, the invoice must be pulled into month one too (billed at close, or the revenue accrued) — margin true both months. Cut-off missed: month one shows ₦1.5m COGS, no revenue (gross margin down ₦2m); month two shows ₦2m revenue, no COGS (margin up ₦2m) — <b>₦4m of margin swing across two months from one document's timing</b>, no error posted anywhere, both months' statements confidently wrong. Multiply by a month-end's normal document traffic and the lesson lands: cut-off is not pedantry; it is whether the monthly P&amp;L means anything at all — and the firms whose margins 'bounce' inexplicably month to month are almost always reading their own cut-off noise.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Reading the P&L", "14", "<p>The first testimony, read aloud. The structure, the reads, and the lies to check for — the script of the monthly MD conversation's first page.</p><p><b>The structure, top to bottom.</b> <b>Revenue</b> — the period's invoiced trade (net of returns and the credit notes that answered them — the selling track's machinery summing). <b>COGS</b> — the delivered goods' carried cost (Inventory 5's valuation releases summing — the line where three tracks meet). <b>Gross margin</b> — the difference and its PERCENTAGE: the trading engine's health in one number, the line every reader checks first. <b>Operating expenses</b> — the running costs, grouped as module 1's chart designed them to be read (the reading list built the groups; the P&amp;L is the reading list answered monthly). <b>Operating profit</b> — what the BUSINESS earned before financing and the state. <b>Below the line</b> — finance costs, the exceptional items (labelled as such, because an exceptional item blended into operations poisons every trend), and the tax charge (the firm's own — module 5's distinction) toward net profit.</p><p><b>The reads, in order of power.</b> <b>Trend before level</b> — this month against the last six (the level says little alone; the gross margin that walked from 24% to 21% over a quarter is a story regardless of whether 21% is 'good'); <b>rate before naira</b> — margins and expense ratios travel across months of different sizes where raw naira mislead; <b>decompose before concluding</b> — the falling gross margin splits (price, mix, or cost: selling cheaper, selling different things, or paying more — three different owners, and the dimension lenses of module 6 do the splitting: margin by branch, by line, the mix visible); and <b>drill before disputing</b> — every line three clicks from its documents (module 1's culture at its monthly exercise: the disputed expense opened live, not argued from the page).</p><p><b>The P&amp;L's lies, checked before reading.</b> The pre-flight list — each lie a module's discipline lapsed: <b>cut-off</b> (Chapter 3 — the bouncing margin's usual author); <b>valuation</b> (Inventory 5 — the COGS that jumped because a bad rate entered the stream, the rate-history chart as the check); <b>the unaccrued month</b> (module 2 — the 'cheap' month that simply hasn't been billed yet); <b>the unaddressed lump</b> (module 6 — branch reads distorted by the default center's residents); and <b>the exceptional blended</b> (the one-off insurance recovery flattering operations). The discipline: the close checklist ran, so the lies were checked structurally — and the reader's residual scepticism is aimed by the checks' outputs (the RNB age, the To-Bill exposure, the lump's size), which the close bundle carries precisely so the reader can calibrate trust line by line.</p><p><b>The conversation's shape.</b> The MD reading, page one: the headline (revenue, gross %, operating profit — three numbers, trended), the two or three lines that MOVED (each with its decomposition and its owner's one-line story — the module 6 variance discipline applied to actuals), the lenses where they add (the branch contribution ranking; the line matrix when it changed), and the ONE decision if the month asks one. Fifteen minutes of the twenty (the balance sheet and cash flow share the rest) — and the ritual's compounding gift is the same as every reading rhythm this academy installed: an owner who reads monthly asks December questions in March, when they are cheap.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Reading the balance sheet: the academy's index", "14", "<p>The second testimony is this academy's own index: every line of a trading firm's balance sheet is an assertion whose machinery some module built, and reading it is walking the curriculum. The structure, then the walk.</p><p><b>The shape.</b> Assets (what the firm HAS): fixed assets (Chapter 7), stock, debtors, prepayments and advances to suppliers, WHT receivable, bank and cash. Liabilities (what it OWES): creditors, customer advances, accrued expenses, the tax custodies, loans. Equity (what remains): capital, retained earnings, the current year's result. The equation holding by grammar (module 1) — and the reading discipline being that the BALANCE is arithmetic while every LINE is a claim requiring its evidence.</p><p><b>The walk — each line's module, and its one-question audit:</b></p><ul><li><b>Stock</b> — Inventory's entire track: is the figure the perpetual ledger's (the stock-to-accounts bridge agreeing — the close checked), at honest cost (module 4's opening law; module 5's method), with the health page's dead-stock and aging reads qualifying HOW GOOD the asset is beyond how big?</li><li><b>Debtors</b> — module 4 of this track: gross, less the provision (shown, not hidden), tied to parties, aged honestly, advances un-netted. The one-question audit: <i>does the aging support the provision, and does the tie hold?</i></li><li><b>Prepayments &amp; supplier advances</b> — modules 2 and 4: the prepaid rent consuming on schedule; the advance aging read clean of immortals.</li><li><b>WHT receivable</b> — module 5: certificated, or a chase in progress — the asset only as real as its evidence file.</li><li><b>Bank &amp; cash</b> — module 3: every balance reconciled THIS month (the reconciliation statements in the bundle), the residues young and named, the floats counted.</li><li><b>Creditors</b> — Procurement 7 and module 4: tied to parties, the RNB's age read, the supplier statement mirrors current for the top names.</li><li><b>Customer advances &amp; accruals</b> — modules 2-4: the liabilities that keep the P&amp;L honest, each with its schedule.</li><li><b>Tax custodies</b> — module 5: at their explainable levels (the zeroing invariant — post-remittance zero or the stated carry-forward), the reserve funded to match.</li><li><b>Equity</b> — the residual, and its own discipline: retained earnings moves ONLY by the year-end closing entry and governed distributions — the unexplained equity movement is Chapter 9's pattern, because equity is where broken books hide their history.</li></ul><p><b>The working capital read.</b> The balance sheet's management story: stock plus debtors minus creditors — the naira the trading cycle TIES UP — read as a trend and against sales (the cycle lengthening is cash quietly burying itself in the yard and the ledger; the academy's tools — the stock ladder, the collections machinery, the payment-terms position — are exactly the levers, and this read is where the owner sees WHY those disciplines were worth installing). Five minutes of the MD conversation: the equation's three components trended, the one that moved decomposed, the lever named. The balance sheet read this way stops being the page owners skip and becomes what it always was: the firm's position, asserted line by line, with every assertion answerable — which is the sentence this academy has been building toward for four tracks.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Cash flow: the third testimony", "13", "<p>The owner's eternal confusion — <i>the P&amp;L says we made money; the bank says we have none</i> — has a statement that answers it, and most trading firms have never read theirs. The third testimony, made usable.</p><p><b>Profit is an opinion; cash is a fact.</b> The confusion's anatomy, taught once: profit books EARNINGS (the invoice raised is revenue — module 1's grammar — whether or not the customer paid), and cash books MOVEMENTS; the gap between them is the balance sheet MOVING — profit that became stock (the margin sitting in the yard as cartons), profit that became debtors (the margin sitting in promises), profit consumed by paying creditors down, profit spent on the new cold room (an asset, not an expense). A profitable firm can die of cash exactly as a cash-rich firm can be quietly unprofitable — and the cash flow statement is the reconciliation of the two stories: it starts from profit and shows, movement by movement, where the money it implies actually went.</p><p><b>The three sections, at working altitude.</b> <b>Operating</b> — the trading engine's cash: profit adjusted for the non-cash lines (depreciation back — it consumed no cash) and the working capital movements (stock up consumes cash; debtors up consumes cash; creditors up releases it — Chapter 5's read, restated as flows); a healthy trading firm generates positive operating cash MOST months, and the profitable firm with chronically negative operating cash is the working-capital squeeze made visible: growing, but burying its margin in stock and debtors faster than it earns it — the read that explains why the growth 'doesn't feel like it'. <b>Investing</b> — the asset spend: the cold room, the vehicles, the fit-outs (Chapter 7's capitalisations landing here as outflows). <b>Financing</b> — the owners' and lenders' money: capital in, loans drawn and repaid, distributions out. Three sections, one story: what the trade generated, what was invested, how the difference was financed — and the month's bank movement reconciling to the three summed, which is the statement proving itself against module 3's witness.</p><p><b>The trading firm's simple read.</b> The consultant's five-minute version for the MD conversation (the formal statement exists; the reading habit needs the plain one): <b>this month's operating cash</b> (did the engine generate or consume?), <b>the working-capital movement decomposed</b> (which component moved — the stock build for December, the debtor who slipped, the creditor stretch), and <b>the runway sentence</b> (the obligations ahead — module 5's compliance calendar, the payment run, payroll — against the position and the reserve: module 3's treasury glance, monthly edition). Where the operating line is negative, the conversation is exactly the academy's toolset: the ladder (release the stock), the chase (collect the debtors), the terms (the payment position both money tracks taught) — the cash flow statement being, in the end, the page that shows which tool this month needs.</p><p><b>The forward read.</b> The statement reports the past; the discipline it feeds looks ahead: the rolling cash view (the calendar's obligations, the expected collections, the planned spends — 60-90 days out) that converts the rare genuine squeeze into module 5's planned conversation instead of a deadline-day scramble. One page, updated at close, read in the same twenty minutes — the third testimony completing the set: what we earned (P&amp;L), where we stand (balance sheet), where the money went and goes (cash flow). An owner fluent in all three is the implementation's finished product.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Fixed assets & depreciation: the long-lived layer", "13", "<p>The last machinery the statements need: the assets that outlive their purchase month — the cold room, the vehicles, the generators, the fit-outs — and the layer that spreads their cost across their lives. The overview a consultant must hold; the policy depths remain the accountant's.</p><p><b>Capitalise or expense: the threshold.</b> The first decision every purchase invoice with a durable item forces: EXPENSE (the cost lands in this month's P&amp;L — correct for consumables and small tools) or CAPITALISE (the cost becomes an ASSET, expensed gradually as depreciation — correct for the durable and material). The instrument is the written <b>capitalisation policy</b>: the value threshold (below it, expense regardless — the ₦40k printer is not worth an asset record's lifetime administration), the category list (what the firm capitalises, into which asset classes, at which useful lives — the accountant's table), and the discipline that the threshold is POLICY, not mood: the year where everything got expensed to dampen a good profit, or capitalised to flatter a bad one, is the provision-dial conviction (module 4) wearing overalls.</p><p><b>The asset register.</b> Capitalised assets live in the register — the asset master (one record per asset: cost, date, class, life, location and COST CENTER — module 6's address reaching the balance sheet, so depreciation lands on the branch that uses the vehicle), and the register's discipline is the item-master law's fourth appearance: created at purchase (the purchase invoice's capitalisation flow feeding it — the asset born from its document, not from a year-end spreadsheet reconstruction), amended as events, and RETIRED by the disposal flow, never deleted. The machinery then does the monthly work: <b>depreciation</b> computed per asset (the method and life from the class — straight-line as the trading firm's workhorse; the accountant owns the choices), posted by the automated entry (DR depreciation expense — addressed by the asset's cost center — CR accumulated depreciation: module 2's lawful catalogue, machine-written), and the balance sheet carrying cost less accumulated depreciation as the net book value the statements show.</p><p><b>Disposals and the physical truth.</b> Assets leave — sold, scrapped, traded in — and the disposal flow closes their story: proceeds against net book value, the gain or loss recognised and LABELLED (Chapter 4's exceptional-items discipline — the generator sold above book flatters no one's operations), the register updated, the accumulated depreciation cleared. And the physical discipline the register makes possible: the <b>asset verification</b> — annually, the register walked against reality (does the asset exist, where the register says, in the condition its value implies?) — Inventory 4's counting logic applied to the long-lived layer, because an asset register never verified accumulates ghosts exactly as an uncounted warehouse does (the vehicle sold informally two years ago, still depreciating; the laptop that left with its user), and the verification is the audit's fixed-asset program pre-run.</p><p><b>CWIP, briefly.</b> The asset being BUILT — the fit-out in progress, the cold room half-installed — parks in <b>capital work-in-progress</b>: costs accumulated, not yet depreciating (depreciation starts at readiness for use, not at first invoice), transferred to the register at commissioning. The discipline is the parking lot's age read (the CWIP that never commissions is either a stalled project or expenses hiding from the P&amp;L — both worth finding), and with it the module's machinery is complete: every balance-sheet line now has its module, and the statements' testimony rests on nothing this academy has not taught.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Year-end, the audit & HealthTrade's close year", "16", "<p>Year-end is the twelfth monthly close plus a short list of annual mechanics — which is the entire point, and HealthTrade's year proves it. The mechanics, then the case.</p><p><b>The year-end's additions.</b> What December carries that other months don't: the <b>closing entries</b> — the fiscal year's P&amp;L accounts closed to retained earnings (the machinery's period-closing voucher doing in one governed motion what the grammar implies: the year's result becoming equity's newest layer — and the ONLY routine way retained earnings ever moves, which is why Chapter 9 convicts the unexplained equity movement); the <b>fiscal year mechanics</b> (the new year opened in the system, the closing performed by the accountant's calendar — the firm's fiscal year needn't be the calendar year, and the system serves either); the <b>annual layer of every module's calendar</b> landing together (the full stock count, the asset verification, the chart and settings walks, the annual returns of module 5 — scheduled ACROSS the season, not stacked into one impossible week); and the <b>audit file handover</b> — which, for a monthly-closing firm, is the year's twelve close bundles plus the annual artifacts, assembled by collation rather than creation: the academy's oldest economics (the system that runs well evidences well) at its largest single payoff.</p><p><b>The audit, met.</b> The external audit's shape against this track's firm: the interim walk-through (the auditor tests the SYSTEM — the controls this academy installed being precisely what they test: the tie, the bridges, the reconciliations, the separations — and a firm that passes walk-through earns sampling instead of substantive excavation, which is the fee conversation in one sentence); the fieldwork (the samples drilled to documents — module 1's culture performing for its most demanding audience); the confirmations (module 4's circularisation, pre-answered); and the <b>adjustments discipline</b>: the auditor's proposed adjustments — there will be some; the accountant agrees or contests them professionally — BOOKED by module 2's doctrine (referenced entries in the current or reopened period per the accountant's formal call, never quiet edits to history), so the audited statements and the ledger end the season as the same document. The relationship note transfers from module 5: the audit is annual and long; the firm whose file answers in a day is the firm whose fee stays reasonable and whose sign-off comes early.</p><p><b>HealthTrade's close year — the case woven.</b> <b>The first close (Ch. 2-3)</b>: twelve days — the backlog's one-time price: the cut-off sweep finds 31 unsubmitted documents (the warehouse's pending receipts the biggest tranche); the To-Bill queue bills ₦1.7m into its rightful month; the checklist runs its first full sequence with three names on its lines. <b>The rhythm (Ch. 2)</b>: close two takes seven days, close four takes five, and by month six the close lands on <b>day four</b> — the consultant audits close four from its file alone and signs the graduation. <b>The readings (Ch. 4-6)</b>: the monthly twenty minutes instituted — month three's session catches the bouncing-margin lesson live (a fat month explained entirely by the prior month's missed To-Bill, the cut-off discipline earning its chapter in front of the MD); month seven's balance-sheet walk surfaces the working-capital trend (the cycle lengthening — stock building ahead of debtor growth), and the cash flow's decomposition hands the month its tool: the ladder runs, Inventory 7's release funding the Akure buildout without a facility. <b>The long-lived layer (Ch. 7)</b>: the register built from the purchase flow (the cold room capitalised with its Akure cost center; depreciation landing on the branch that runs it), the first asset verification retiring two ghosts (a disposed generator still depreciating — corrected by doctrine, labelled exceptional). <b>The year-end (this chapter)</b>: December closes on day five like any month; the annual layer runs across six scheduled weeks; the closing entry moves the year's result to retained earnings in one governed motion; and the audit — fourteen customer confirmations, twelve folders handed over, walk-through passed — signs off in <b>half the prior year's time</b> with four small adjustments, each booked by doctrine. The accountant's year-end line joins the academy's collection: <i>December was the twelfth boring month — which, I now understand, was the whole idea.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the seventh law", "13", "<p>The close-and-statements layer's scar tissue. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the never-closed month.</b> Symptom: periods open for a year; statements 'as at' dates nobody trusts; every report a draft. Disease: the ritual never instituted (Ch. 1-2) — the firm trading on approximately-narrated books. Fix: the first-close engagement (the backlog priced once, honestly), the checklist with names, the day-five target, and the freeze making each sealed month stay sealed.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the year-end scramble.</b> Symptom: January archaeology; twelve months of accruals in one storm; statements about a year nobody remembers. Disease: closing annually what should close monthly (Ch. 1) — every error twelve months stale by discovery. Fix: the monthly rhythm, which dissolves this pattern by construction — the storm-into-weather trade, this track's edition.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the bouncing margin.</b> Symptom: gross margin swinging month to month on stable trade; owners distrusting their own P&amp;L. Disease: cut-off noise read as news (Ch. 3) — the completeness pairs breaking at every boundary. Fix: the day-one document sweep, the To-Bill queues read in naira at every close, the RNB age watched — and the whiteboard minute that teaches why one document's timing swings margin twice.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — statements nobody reads.</b> Symptom: the pages produced, circulated, filed — and no decision ever traced to them; the owner learning the firm's position from the bank app. Disease: testimony without an audience (Ch. 1, 4-6) — the reading habit never installed. Fix: the twenty-minute MD reading as a standing ritual, the scripts of Chapters 4-6, the three-number headline discipline — and the consultant modelling it until the owner runs it alone.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the unexplained equity movement.</b> Symptom: retained earnings shifting between periods with no closing entry behind it; equity as a plug for whatever wouldn't balance. Disease: the gravest here — history edited at its last hiding place (Ch. 5, 8): deleted documents, forced balances, and migration sins all wash up in equity, because equity is where broken books bury their dead. Fix: the excavation (each movement traced to its cause — the GL always knows), the closing entry as retained earnings' only routine mover, deletion rights removed, and the equity line's stability watched as the books' deepest health signal.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the ghost register.</b> Symptom: assets depreciating years after disposal; the register a year-end spreadsheet nobody reconciles; net book values nobody would bet on. Disease: the long-lived layer run on memory (Ch. 7). Fix: assets born from their purchase documents, disposals through the flow, the annual verification retiring ghosts — the counting discipline applied to the slowest-moving stock the firm owns.</p><p><b>The seventh law.</b> The chart is the language; the journal is the exception; the bank is the outside witness; the controls tie; tax accounts are custody; profit has an address. The seventh is the destination's own: <b>the close is a ritual and the statements are its testimony — close every month in order, read what you close with the owner, and let December be the twelfth boring month.</b> The checklist assembles every module's bridge and sweep; cut-off keeps the margin meaning something; the three testimonies are read, not filed; the long-lived layer counts its ghosts; and the year-end arrives as a formality because twelve sealed months preceded it. One module remains: the structure that holds the whole finance function — settings, roles, the master calendar, and the handover test that closes the certification.</p>"),
]

